      "text": "To find the volume of a sphere inscribed in a cube, we first need to understand the relationship between the dimensions of the cube and the sphere.\n\n1. **Understanding the relationship:**\n   - The sphere has a radius equal to the edge length of the cube.\n\n2. **Given values:**\n   - The edge length of the cube \\( a = 6 \\) inches.\n\n3. **Sphere's relationship to the cube:**\n   - Since the sphere is inscribed in the cube, the diameter of the sphere will be equal to the edge length of the cube.\n\n4. **Determine the radius of the sphere:**\n   - Diameter \\( D = 2 \\times \\text{radius} \\).\n\n5. **Calculate the radius:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Radius} = \\frac{D}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Using the volume formula for a sphere:**\n   - The volume \\( V \\) of a sphere is given by:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n   \\]\n\n7. **Substitute the radius into the formula:**\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (3)^3\n   \\]\n\n8. **Calculate the cube of the radius:**\n   \\[\n   3^3 = 27\n   \\]\n\n9. **Multiply by \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi\\):**\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 27 \\times \\pi\n   \\]\n\n10. **Simplify the expression:**\n    \\[\n    V = 36 \\pi\n    \\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe volume of the inscribed sphere is \\(\\boxed{36\\pi}\\) cubic inches.",
